    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d495">

                <pb facs="0948" n="824" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg824"/>

                <head>No. 491.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Señor Navarro.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-01-14"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 14,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your two notes, dated, respectively, November 15 last and the 27th ultimo, in
                    which further complaints are made against a band of thieves, headed by one
                    Robert E. Martin, whose depredations upon citizens of Mexico this government
                    seriously regrets and hopes to prevent.</p>
                <p>To this end copies of your notes have been forwarded to his excellency the
                    governor of Texas, with a request that whatever measures may lie within his
                    excellency’s power for the arrest and punishment of the guilty persons may not
                    be spared, as well to protect our interests as those of Mexico, which have
                    suffered by their acts, by the arrest and punishment of the guilty parties.</p>
                <p>Accept, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
